<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bolkiah</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. c. 1485–<a href="/years/1524">1524</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Sultan Bolkiah, Nakhoda Ragam</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bolkiah was the fifth sultan of Brunei, who reigned from about 1485 to 1524. Remembered in legend as Nakhoda Ragam, the singing captain, he led naval expeditions that extended Brunei's power over much of the coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borneo">Borneo</a> and into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a>, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manila">Manila</a>. His reign is regarded as Brunei's golden age, and his tomb is at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kota-batu">Kota Batu</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bolkiah.jpg" alt="Bolkiah" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bubbha at English Wikipedia · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bolkiah-tomb.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Bolkiah was the son of Sultan Sulaiman and came to the throne in 1485 when his father abdicated. In Bornean and Malay tradition he bears the title Nakhoda Ragam, the singing captain, though it has been argued that the title was also given to another Sultan of Brunei and to other figures of the Malay Archipelago. He was said to enjoy playing the lute and drums, and stories remember him as an adventurous navigator who often travelled to inspect his lands. He appears in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batu-tarsilah">Batu Tarsilah</a> and the Silsilah Raja-Raja Berunai, both 19th-century genealogies, and the Spanish Boxer Codex calls him Sultan Salan.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>The royal genealogies remember Bolkiah as the sultan who defeated the states of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suluk">Suluk</a> and Seludang, and his rule is said to have reached present-day Sarawak and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sabah">Sabah</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manila">Manila</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulu-archipelago">Sulu Archipelago</a>, possibly with parts of Kalimantan as far as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banjarmasin">Banjarmasin</a>. Through his marriage to Puteri Lela Menchanai, a princess of Sulu, Brunei's influence and the spread of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> in the region grew further. He is considered the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> of Brunei to have used <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannons</a>, having paid forty Javanese blacksmiths to teach metal casting.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonio-pigafetta">Antonio Pigafetta</a>, who came to Brunei in 1521 with the ships of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-magellan">Ferdinand Magellan</a>'s expedition under Gonzalo de Espinosa, described a rich court where envoys spoke to the ruler only through a chain of officials. He also recorded a fleet of more than a hundred boats returning from a campaign led by the prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luzon">Luzon</a>, which had restored Brunei's rule over Laoe, or Labai, in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borneo">Borneo</a>. Espinosa, fearing an attack, fired on the junks and sank two before he learnt his mistake and apologised. Pigafetta's report bears out the Bruneian oral tradition about the size of the realm.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Bolkiah died on 17 July 1524 and was succeeded by his son Abdul Kahar. His sandstone mausoleum stands on a ridge at Jalan Kota Batu above the Brunei River, two tombstones on eight tiers of carved stone. The tomb itself is unmarked: two steles, probably of the late 17th century, commemorate Sultan Muhammad Ali and Sultan Abdul Mubin, and a simpler slab between them gives Bolkiah's death date, perhaps carved later to support <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hugh-low">Hugh Low</a>'s suggestion that Magellan's expedition came late in Bolkiah's reign. A campus of the Institute of Brunei Technical Education in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seria">Seria</a> and Kampong Bolkiah in Bandar Seri Begawan are named after him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bolkiah” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Brunei tradition credits the fifth sultan, <b>Bolkiah</b>, remembered as Nakhoda Ragam, the 'singing captain', with expeditions that extended Brunei's influence over the coasts of Borneo, Sulu and the Manila region in the late fifteenth and early sixteenth centuries.</blockquote><a href="/empire/brunei-sultanate">Brunei Sultanate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Tradition holds that around 1500 <b>Sultan Bolkiah</b> of Brunei conquered it and set up a ruling family allied to Brunei, beginning with Rajah Salalila.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-maynila">Kingdom of Maynila</a></li></ul></section>
